course: dictionary
lesson: 6898
title: Overwork and overworked
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Overwork means working too hard or too long, so you get tired. «Tired, overworked muscles cramp more easily» shows how too much work can wear the body out. Rest is the cure.
explain: Overwork can also be a noun: too much work that makes someone tired or ill. Overworked is an adjective, so an overworked worker is tired from too much work. Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing.
explain: Overwork can also mean making someone else work too hard. Sometimes a word itself gets overworked: it is used so often that it becomes boring, like "nice" or "said". «Overworking means adding too much until the picture gets muddy» is about paint, and painters step back to stop it.
fact: Overwork (verb) means to work too hard or too long, or to make someone do so.
fact: Overwork (noun) means too much work that makes someone tired or ill.
fact: «Overworked» can mean tired from too much work.
fact: An overworked word is used so often that it is boring, such as "nice" or "said".
fact: Context clue: «Tired, overworked muscles cramp more easily» tells you overwork makes people and muscles tired.
exercise: «Tired, overworked muscles cramp more easily.» What does overworked mean here? | Newly painted | Very happy and relaxed | Full of words | * Tired from too much work
exercise: Teacher says «nice» is an overworked word. What does this mean? | It is the longest word in the lesson | * It is used so often that it is boring | It means very kind | It means too much work
exercise: Which is the best meaning of overwork as a verb? | * To work too hard or too long | To rest for a long time | To sleep at school | To paint a picture
test: «Overworking means adding too much until the picture gets muddy.» What happens when a painter overworks a picture? | It is finished in one minute | * It gets muddy | It gets bigger | It gets dry and clean
test: Which word means too much work that makes someone tired or ill? | rest | painting | distance | * overwork
exam: Ella says overwork can be a noun and a verb. Is she right? | No, it is only a sound | No, it is only a noun | * Yes, it can be both | No, it is only an adjective
